## 1. WHAT CAREWORK IS (and why)

CareWork is a **multi-tenant SaaS platform** for the Australian disability and aged-care sector. It competes with Mable, HireUp, and LikeFamily — but with two key differences:

1. **Registered-provider-grade compliance** — we keep full legal responsibility for compliance (unlike Mable, which shifts liability to contractors).
2. **Wellness-integrated care** — matching by shared interests and monitoring worker burnout, not just skills and availability.

**Pilot phase:** HelpHome (the existing NDIS provider) is **tenant #1** — the first coordinator organisation using the platform. Other coordinators (Jewish Care–style orgs) can join as additional tenants.

**Why build it (the story):**

- HelpHome uses VisualCare as its operational backbone (rostering, compliance, Xero). Client portal costs extra and wasn't licensed.
- Existing clients have no self-service way to book support — they call/email Tonia.
- Workers have no way to flag burnout before they quit.
- Competitors charge 17–33% platform fees. We charge 0% on the worker rate (subscription model).

---

## 2. THE PROBLEM WE'RE SOLVING

| Problem | Who feels it | How CareWork helps |
|---|---|---|
| Clients call/email to book shifts | Tonia + coordinators | 24/7 self-service portal |
| No interest-based matching | Clients | Workers matched on hobbies/goals |
| Workers burn out and quit | Coordinators | Confidential wellbeing check-ins + alerts |
| No visibility of worker load | Coordinators | Real-time capacity bars + derived status |
| Expensive platform fees | Everyone | 0% transaction fees; subscription model |

---

## 3. THE BUSINESS MODEL

- **New company** owns the platform (CareWork) — same owners as HelpHome.
- **Multi-tenant** — each coordinator organisation is a tenant. Data is siloed.
- **Revenue streams:**
  - Subscription: clients pay premium tier (currently $19/mo, hidden during pilot)
  - Future: B2B placement fees from coordinators
  - Future: Stripe/Xero integration for real payments (post-pilot)
- **Workers keep 100%** of their rate — no per-booking deduction.
- **Registered-provider path:** HelpHome is unregistered today (claims via PlanCare). Registration expected next year enables direct NDIS/HCP claims.

---

## 4. THE ROLES

Four roles. Each sees only their own world.

### 4.1 Client

- **Sees:** Dashboard (workers, filters, booking), Wellness check-in
- **Can:** Browse independent workers, book shifts, log mood/goals/satisfaction
- **Cannot:** See coordinator workers, see other clients' data, access admin pages
- **Demo login:** `jane.doe@client.com` / `test`

### 4.2 Worker

Two sub-types:

**Independent** (marketplace contractor):

- **Sees:** My Hub with one tab: CareWork Marketplace (no tab switcher shown)
- **Can:** Accept/decline marketplace requests, submit wellbeing check-ins
- **Cannot:** See roster shifts (they have no coordinator), access coordinator/admin

**Coordinator** (belongs to a coordinator org, e.g. HelpHome):

- **Sees:** My Hub with HelpHome Roster tab (plus Marketplace if dual)
- **Can:** Same as independent + see assigned shifts from their coordinator
- **Cannot:** See other workers' data, access admin pages

**Dual worker** (both types):

- **Sees:** Two tabs — CareWork Marketplace + HelpHome Roster
- **Demo login:** `sarah.johnson@helphome-worker.com` / `test`

### 4.3 Coordinator

- **Sees:** Coordinator Hub (roster of assigned workers with wellbeing status), Verification Queue
- **Can:** Filter by wellbeing status, schedule welfare chats, verify/revoke worker documents, see client requests for their workers
- **Cannot:** See other tenants' workers, access admin-only pages
- **Demo login:** `tonia@helphome.au` / `test`

### 4.4 Admin (CareWork platform super-admin)

- **Sees:** Everything — all tenants, all workers, all clients
- **Can:** All coordinator actions + see all client requests across the platform, view platform KPIs/charts/alerts/activity
- **Cannot:** Nothing restricted (it's the top-level role)
- **Demo login:** `admin@carework.au` / `test`

---

## 5. THE STACK (and why each choice)

| Layer | Technology | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Frontend** | React 18 + Vite + TypeScript | Fast, standard, huge ecosystem |
| **Styling** | Custom CSS (index.css) | Full brand control, no framework overhead |
| **Routing** | React Router 6 | Standard SPA routing |
| **API** | Hono (TypeScript) | Runtime-agnostic — runs on Workers, Node, Bun, Deno, Azure |
| **Worker runtime** | Cloudflare Workers | Free tier, global edge, integrated with Pages |
| **Database** | Supabase Postgres (Sydney) | Real Postgres, AU residency, free tier |
| **ORM** | Drizzle | Type-safe, portable to any Postgres |
| **Postgres driver** | `postgres` (porsager) | Works in both Node AND Workers (unlike `pg`) |
| **Connection pooling** | Cloudflare Hyperdrive | Required for Workers → external Postgres |
| **Frontend hosting** | Cloudflare Pages | Free static hosting |
| **Proxy layer** | Pages Functions | Bridges browser ↔ Worker (no CORS drama) |
| **Testing** | Playwright (UI) + Node fetch (API) | Headless Chrome for real interaction tests |
| **Source control** | GitHub (`ntchita/helphome`) | Standard |

**Why this specific combination:**

- **Hono** was chosen because the same code runs on Cloudflare Workers today and Azure tomorrow (only the adapter changes).
- **`postgres` driver** instead of `pg` because `pg` doesn't work in Workers (no TCP).
- **Hyperdrive** because Workers can't hold raw Postgres connections — Hyperdrive pools them.
- **Supabase** because it's the closest free-tier to Azure Postgres we'll eventually migrate to — no code changes needed, just a new connection string.

---

## 6. THE ARCHITECTURE

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  BROWSER (desktop / mobile)                                 │
└──────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┘
                           │ HTTPS
                           ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CLOUDFLARE PAGES  (helphome-app.pages.dev)                 │
│  ├── React SPA (compiled from src/frontend/)                │
│  └── Pages Function (functions/api/[[path]].ts)             │
│      → forwards /api/* to the Worker                        │
└──────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┘
                           │ HTTPS (server-to-server)
                           ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CLOUDFLARE WORKER  (helphome-api.lifewealth.workers.dev)   │
│  Hono API — all business logic                              │
│  ├── Auth middleware (x-user-id header OR Bearer JWT)       │
│  ├── Role guards (client/worker/coordinator/admin)          │
│  └── 35+ routes (see §8)                                    │
└──────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┘
                           │ Hyperdrive binding
                           ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  SUPABASE POSTGRES  (Sydney, ap-southeast-2)                │
│  14 tables, multi-tenant via tenant_id                      │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

**Why the proxy layer exists:**

Without it, the browser would need to hit the Worker directly from a different origin, requiring careful CORS config on every request. With the proxy:

- Browser sees only `helphome-app.pages.dev`
- No CORS in the browser (same-origin)
- Proxy forwards to the Worker with clean headers
- Worker sees a plain server-to-server call

**Security boundary:**

- Browser NEVER talks to Supabase directly
- Supabase password exists only in two places: local `.dev.vars` and Cloudflare Worker secret
- All data access goes through the Worker (which enforces role guards)

