## 11. LOCAL DEVELOPMENT

### One-time setup

```bash
cd ~/helphome-platform/helphome
npm install
```

Create `.dev.vars` at repo root (gitignored):

```
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.abjdnryzywhvndldvwgh:<PASSWORD>@aws-0-ap-southeast-2.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres
```

Verify it's gitignored:

```bash
git check-ignore .dev.vars     # should print .dev.vars
```

### Run

Two Git Bash windows, both from repo root:

**Window 1 — backend:**

```bash
node server.js
```

→ http://localhost:8787

**Window 2 — frontend:**

```bash
npx vite
```

→ http://localhost:5173

### DB mode

- **With `DATABASE_URL` set** → uses Supabase (production parity)
- **Without** → falls back to PGlite `.data/pg` (offline, zero setup)
- To force PGlite: temporarily rename `.dev.vars`.

> **Important gotcha:** `drizzle-kit push` uses the **`pg`** driver and will target whatever `DATABASE_URL` points to (usually Supabase). Your **running backend** may be using PGlite at `.data/pg` instead. They are different databases — a column added by `push` to Supabase will NOT exist in PGlite. To check which DB the running process is using:

```bash
node -e "console.log(process.env.DATABASE_URL ? 'Supabase' : 'PGlite')"
```

### Common issues

| Error | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| EADDRINUSE :8787 | Backend already running | Ctrl+C old window |
| Cannot find module | node_modules missing | `npm install` |
| ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND on .ts import | Missing .ts extension in relative import | Add `.ts` |
| PGlite Aborted() error | Corrupted DB from force-kill | See §15 |
| 500 on /api/login | Session cache in Worker | Redeploy Worker (§12) |
| `column "worker_profiles"."consent_to_display" does not exist` | Running backend is on PGlite, but migration was applied to Supabase (or vice versa) | Confirm which DB the backend is using (see above); apply the column to the correct DB; restart backend |
| `syntax error at or near "="` from PGlite | Drizzle generated `eq(undefined, value)` — usually a column missing from schema.ts | Add the column to `schema.ts`, restart backend |

---

## 12. DEPLOYMENT

### 12.1 Deploy the API (Cloudflare Worker)

```bash
npx wrangler deploy
```

→ updates `helphome-api.lifewealth.workers.dev`

Requires `wrangler.toml` to have the Hyperdrive binding:

```toml
name = "helphome-api"
main = "src/api/index.ts"
compatibility_date = "2025-09-01"
compatibility_flags = ["nodejs_compat"]

[vars]
ENVIRONMENT = "production"

[[hyperdrive]]
binding = "HYPERDRIVE"
id = "f9dfbabe72104e969a7caa16fdf75769"

[dev]
port = 8787
```

Requires the Worker secret `DATABASE_URL` (set once via `npx wrangler secret put DATABASE_URL`).

### 12.2 Deploy the Frontend (Cloudflare Pages)

```bash
npm run build
cp -r functions src/functions
npx wrangler pages deploy src/dist --project-name helphome-app --branch main
```

**Why the `cp -r`:** Pages looks for `functions/` next to the deploy directory. We deploy from `src/dist/` so functions must be at `src/functions/`. Permanent fix pending (change Vite `outDir` to root `dist/`).

### 12.3 Run migrations

```bash
npx drizzle-kit generate    # create migration file
npx drizzle-kit push        # apply to Supabase (dev only — always review before prod)
```

> **Warning:** Always review generated migrations before applying. Migration `0007_famous_loa.sql` was generated containing `DROP COLUMN "consent_to_display"` — the drop was unintentional and cost a debugging session. Scan every generated file for `DROP`.

### 12.4 Deploy secrets

```bash
npx wrangler secret put DATABASE_URL    # paste Supabase connection string when prompted
```

### 12.5 What to do BEFORE every deploy

1. Run both smoke suites locally (§13)
2. Commit and push all changes
3. Deploy API first, then Frontend
4. Run both smoke suites against production
5. If broken → rollback (§15)

---

## 13. AUTOMATED TESTS

### API smoke — 37 checks, ~2 seconds

```bash
# Local
node scripts/smoke.mjs

# Production
SMOKE_API=https://helphome-api.lifewealth.workers.dev node scripts/smoke.mjs
```

Covers: login for all 4 roles, worker list, booking, wellness, worker hub, checkins, decisions, flag-support, roster, requests, verification, admin endpoints, charts, register dedup, auth guards, full auth lifecycle (register → verify → login → forgot → reset).

### UI smoke — 64 checks, ~2 minutes (Playwright headless Chrome)

```bash
# Local (both servers must be running)
node scripts/ui-smoke.mjs

# Production
SMOKE_BASE=https://helphome-app.pages.dev \
SMOKE_API=https://helphome-api.lifewealth.workers.dev \
node scripts/ui-smoke.mjs
```

Covers: real browser interactions — login flows for all roles, filter chips that narrow grids, Book Now button state flip, **worker tab switching (independent vs dual)**, welfare chat, verification toggle, register all 3 doors, mobile hamburger, session expiry bounce, forgot/reset password UI.

> **Warning:** UI smoke writes real data to the DB (bookings, wellness logs, signup leads, welfare chats, verification toggles). For pilot phase OK. For public launch: run against a staging DB.

**Note:** `ui-smoke.mjs` calls `POST /api/_test/complete-onboarding` at start so demo workers skip the `/onboarding` redirect introduced in Part B and reach `/my-hub` where the tests expect them.

